    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d522">

                <pb facs="0599" n="533" sameAs="frus:frus1934v05/pg533"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">817.00/7942: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Nicaragua</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Lane</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                        State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Managua</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1934-02-23T17:00:00-05:45">February 23, 1934—5
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received 6:45 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>62. There have been no disorders in Managua following death of Sandino, although
                    situation is still somewhat tense with state of siege in force and telegraph and
                    telephone communications restricted. <pb facs="0600" n="534" xml:id="pg534"/>
                    Granada is quiet according to a reliable report, and American Consul at Puerto
                    Cabezas reports everything quiet in that region.</p>
                <p>Repeated to Central American Missions.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Lane</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
